<question type="stack">
  <name><text>Ch09_P03_Spoil_the_first_proof</text></name>
  <questiontext format="html"><text><![CDATA[<p>In the first attempt, \(f(n) = an^2 + bn + c\) and \(f(c) = c(ac + b + 1)\).</p>
<p>(a) Give an example of a quadratic \(f(n)\), with whole-number coefficients, for which \(f(c)\) is prime. Then the attempt is wrong for that quadratic. Type, for example, <code>2*n^2 + 3*n + 5</code>.</p>
<div>\(f(n) =\) [[input:ans1]] [[validation:ans1]] [[feedback:prt1]]</div>
<p>(b) The attempt claims that \(c(ac + b + 1)\) cannot be prime because it has the two factors \(c\) and \(ac + b + 1\). In which of these cases can \(c(ac + b + 1)\) still be prime? Select all that apply.</p>
<div>[[input:ans2]] [[validation:ans2]]</div>
[[feedback:prt2]]]]></text></questiontext>
  <generalfeedback format="html"><text><![CDATA[<p>A prime number is a whole number greater than 1 whose only positive divisors are 1 and itself. So a prime \(p\) does have two factors, 1 and \(p\). The first attempt forgets this: \(f(c) = c(ac + b + 1)\) can still be prime when one of its factors is 1 or \(-1\).</p>
<p>For example: \(f(n) = n^2 + 1\) has \(c = 1\) and \(f(1) = 2\); \(f(n) = n^2 - 3n + 3\) has \(ac + b + 1 = 1\) and \(f(3) = 3\); \(f(n) = 4n^2 - 1\) has \(c = -1\) and \(f(-1) = 3\).</p>
<p>When \(c = 0\), \(f(c) = 0\), and when both factors are at least 2, \(f(c)\) has a factor other than 1 and itself: in those cases the attempt’s step is right. But a proof must work for every quadratic, so the attempt fails.</p>]]></text></generalfeedback>
